</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>The Effect of Different Linear Transforms</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we ablate various choices of transforms adopted for outlier mitigation of outliers. Specifically, we consider the following options:</p>
<ul id="A7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Identity transform.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hadamard rotation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Grouped Sequency-arranged Rotation (GSR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We sweep over different options of transform sizes (<math id="A7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{16,32,64,128,256\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>128</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{16,32,64,128,256\}</annotation></semantics></math>) both for NVFP and MXFP formats.
The average score on 5 tasks from LM Evaluation Harness (piqa, winogrande, hellaswag, arc-easy, arc-challenge) is reported.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From these results, one can observe that rotations yield small improvement relative to identity transform for MXFP format and minor degradation for NVFP with RTN quantization. Different transform sizes perform more or less the same.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T12" class="ltx_table">
<div id="A7.T12.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:403pt;vertical-align:-199.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-66.8pt,67.7pt) scale(0.748518792250874,0.748518792250874) ;">
